From 391d70eb80a9b412e59699537420e0d8d447033d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ebg-ant <296364998+ebg-ant@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 14:56:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] perf: a floating window that comes back where it was is
 foretold too

Since a floating window's frame is its person's, one that comes back into view is put back
where it was (`.putBack`) and no longer placed. Left out of the forecast, `back` and
`show-all` on a desk of thirty made seven windows wait for one another again. What is
foretold is `putBack`'s own first request: nothing while Arbor stops without moving, nothing
for a window that is there, in full screen or in a hand, and nothing for one that has to be
sized somewhere else first, which goes there after a look at the Dock. A test puts each case
to both, the forecast and `putBack`. Its five conditions were taken out in six ways, and each
turned it red.

What the trunk had changed under the tests:
- A drop that is only tried out pushes floating windows aside only where they make way, and
  needs a Desktop beside two docked places: the test of it is pinned to both.
- So is the test of a layout that is reckoned again. Since windows that may refuse are asked
  before anybody is moved, its window never refused after the forecast, and "at most one
  move" held of floating windows that did not move at all. Now the window has been seen as
  narrow as its slot and its app has changed its mind since, so it refuses in its slot,
  unasked; and a floating window must follow. Foretold from the layout as first reckoned,
  the test goes red again.
- Every other twin walk begins with both settings on, which also walks the road on which
  floating windows are placed.
- A start asks the window server what still exists three times, not two
  (`noteTheWindowsTheFileKnew` is new).
